# 3. Candidate Lemma 1 — Persistent-Corridor Charging Lemma

This is currently the most promising candidate.

## Candidate Lemma 1

Suppose \(T\) cannot be burned in \(q\) rounds.

Choose a failed \(q\)-round burning sequence that is maximal in some natural sense, for example lexicographically maximizing the number of covered vertices after each round.

Then conjecturally there is a sequence of persistent obstruction corridors

$$
C_1,\ldots,C_q
$$

such that:

* the first \(q-1\) persistent corridors each contain at least two degree-2 vertices;
* the final obstruction requires at least one additional degree-2 vertex;
* the relevant degree-2 vertices can be charged disjointly.

This would give

$$
n_2(T)\ge
2(q-1)+1
=
2q-1.
$$

### Why this might imply the target

If

$$
n_2(T)\le2q-2,
$$

then there would not be enough degree-2 vertices to sustain \(q\) layers of persistent obstruction.

Hence a \(q\)-round covering would have to exist.

### [STRUCTURAL HYPOTHESIS]

The intended mechanism is that a genuinely persistent unburned region needs a degree-2 corridor to shield it from branching.

A branching vertex exposes several directions simultaneously to a burning ball, whereas a degree-2 corridor behaves like a one-dimensional bottleneck.

Thus one might hope that each additional round of persistence consumes two fresh degree-2 vertices, with a final terminal obstruction requiring one more.

This naturally produces

$$
2(q-1)+1=2q-1.
$$

### Stress tests

**Paths.**

Paths contain many degree-2 vertices, so they are compatible with the corridor picture. Their burning number is nevertheless only \(\lceil\sqrt n\rceil\), so the existence of corridors is clearly not sufficient for being an obstruction.

**Stars.**

Stars have \(n_2=0\) and very small burning number. This is consistent with the idea that branching makes persistent obstruction difficult.

**Double stars and highly branched trees.**

[STRUCTURAL HYPOTHESIS]

The abundance of branching should make it easier to reroute or place burning sources effectively, again favoring the proposed mechanism.

**Balanced subdivided stars.**

The \(K_{1,9}\) experiments show that sufficiently long balanced arms can eventually produce large burning numbers. This is consistent with the need for persistent corridors.

### Weakness

The crucial statement

$$
\text{one additional persistent round}
\Longrightarrow
\text{two new degree-2 vertices}
$$

is completely unproved.

Possible failures include:

* the same degree-2 vertex being useful in several rounds;
* different obstruction corridors overlapping;
* branching vertices participating in a genuine timing obstruction;
* source timing creating a global obstruction that cannot be localized to one corridor.

