# 4. Candidate Lemma 2 — Branching-Compensation Lemma

A substantially different approach is to first prove that branching cannot sustain a minimal obstruction.

## Candidate Lemma 2

Consider a minimal failed \(q\)-round burning strategy.

Suppose a persistent unburned component has a boundary containing a branching vertex \(v\).

Then conjecturally one can move some later burning source to \(v\), or otherwise reroute the source sequence, without decreasing the eventual number of covered vertices.

Therefore a minimal persistent obstruction should have its active boundary inside degree-2 paths rather than at branching vertices.

Symbolically:

$$
\boxed{
\text{persistent obstruction}
\Rightarrow
\text{degree-2 corridor boundary}.
}
$$

### Why it is attractive

This reduces the global tree to a much simpler local object:

$$
\text{branching structure}
\longrightarrow
\text{source rerouting}
\longrightarrow
\text{degree-2 corridor}
\longrightarrow
\text{charging argument}.
$$

In other words, Candidate 2 could provide the missing first step needed for Candidate 1.

### Stress tests

**Stars.**

The center is obviously the highest-value source, so the proposed compensation mechanism is compatible with the simplest branching example.

**Subdivided stars.**

The center provides efficient access to many arms, while the genuine difficulty occurs farther along the subdivided branches.

**Highly branched trees.**

[STRUCTURAL HYPOTHESIS]

More branching should provide more opportunities for source replacement, making persistent obstructions harder to sustain.

### Main weakness

A local source move might preserve the current round's coverage but destroy an optimal future schedule.

Thus

$$
\text{local improvement}
\not\Rightarrow
\text{global preservation}
$$

without an additional argument.

