# 5. Candidate Lemma 3 — Small Topological Witness Lemma

A third approach is to work directly with the suppressed core.

## Candidate Lemma 3

Let \(H\) be the degree-2-suppressed core of a minimal \(q\)-obstruction.

Conjecturally there exists a connected core subtree \(K\subseteq H\) that carries all of the essential obstruction and satisfies

$$
\sum_{e\in E(K)}s_e\ge2q-1.
$$

Then automatically

$$
n_2(T)
=
\sum_{e\in E(H)}s_e
\ge2q-1.
$$

### Why it may be useful

This converts the problem into finding a small topological witness carrying enough subdivision mass.

The \(K_{1,9}\) computations suggest that a relatively small set of core edges can control the increase in burning number. For example, the first \(b=4\) and \(b=5\) examples use balanced groups of only three and four long arms.

### Weakness

The boundary data show substantial diversity in the suppressed cores.

Therefore there is no evidence that the witness must be a star, or even that its core must have a particularly simple form.

The safe version is therefore:

> a small obstruction witness may exist,

not

> every obstruction is a subdivided star.

