# 6. Best Current Candidate

The strongest candidate is a combination of Candidates 2 and 1.

## Proposed research target

### [STRUCTURAL HYPOTHESIS]

**Step A — Branching compensation.**

In a minimal failed \(q\)-round strategy, persistent unburned components can be arranged so that their relevant boundaries do not contain branching vertices.

### [STRUCTURAL HYPOTHESIS]

**Step B — Corridor persistence.**

Once the obstruction is confined to degree-2 paths, every additional round of persistence requires at least two fresh degree-2 vertices.

### [STRUCTURAL HYPOTHESIS]

**Step C — Terminal charge.**

After \(q-1\) such persistence transitions, the final unresolved component requires at least one additional degree-2 vertex.

Hence

$$
n_2(T)\ge
2(q-1)+1
=
2q-1.
$$

This is precise enough that the individual steps can be attacked separately.

---

# 7. Why \(2q-1\) Appears Naturally

### [STRUCTURAL HYPOTHESIS]

The most appealing feature of this approach is that the number \(2q-1\) arises as

$$
\boxed{2(q-1)+1}.
$$

A possible interpretation is:

$$
\underbrace{2+2+\cdots+2}_{q-1\text{ persistence layers}}
+\underbrace{1}_{\text{final terminal obstruction}}.
$$

Thus the threshold is not being inserted artificially; it emerges from a possible temporal charging process.

This is presently only a proposed mechanism.

