# 9. Potential Failure Modes

A serious attempt should actively try to break the proposed mechanism.

### Failure mode 1

A branching vertex may be part of a genuinely optimal timing obstruction.

Then Candidate 2 is false.

### Failure mode 2

A single degree-2 vertex may serve as a witness for several persistence layers.

Then the simple \(2(q-1)\) charging bound fails.

### Failure mode 3

Different persistent regions may share the same degree-2 corridor.

Then a naive charging argument double-counts degree-2 vertices.

### Failure mode 4

There may exist a highly branched obstruction with very few degree-2 vertices but a complicated global source-timing constraint.

Then the corridor mechanism is incomplete.

These failure modes should be treated as targets for counterexample searches, not ignored.

